format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

objective u4-three-questions

statement:
    Know three questions to ask about any dose in a headline: how big it is in millisieverts, how fast it arrived, and how it compares with doses you know.

misconception: A scary headline about radiation tells you how big the dose is.

item u4-l5-choice-compare
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

prompt:
    A report says a scan gave a dose of about 10 millisieverts. How does that compare with an average year, about 6, and with about 1000?

options:
    {"id":"mid","label":"A little more than an average year, and about a hundred times less than 1000"}
    {"id":"huge","label":"Far more than 1000, so a scan is dangerous","note":"10 is far less than 1000. About 1000 in a short time is where illness starts."}
    {"id":"tiny","label":"Far less than an average year","note":"10 is a little more than the average year's 6, not far less."}

answer: mid

explanation:
    10 is a little more than an average year's 6, and about a hundred times less than the 1000 where people start to become ill.

item u4-l5-choice-fast
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

prompt:
    Two people each receive 10 millisieverts. One got it in a few minutes, the other over a whole year. Which question tells them apart?

options:
    {"id":"rate","label":"How fast did the dose arrive?"}
    {"id":"size","label":"How big was the dose?","note":"Both received the same size, 10 millisieverts. The question that tells them apart is about speed."}
    {"id":"nat","label":"Is the radiation natural?","note":"That is a different question and does not tell these two apart."}

answer: rate

explanation:
    The size is the same, so the difference is the dose rate: how fast the dose arrived.

item u4-l5-choice-first
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

prompt:
    A headline says 'Radiation found in the air!' and gives no numbers. What should you ask first?

options:
    {"id":"dose","label":"How big is the dose, and how does it compare with an ordinary year?"}
    {"id":"any","label":"Nothing: if radiation is found, it is dangerous","note":"Radiation is around us all the time. What matters is the size and the speed of the dose."}
    {"id":"nat","label":"Only whether the radiation is natural or made by people","note":"Natural or not, the dose is what matters. Most of the average yearly dose is natural."}

answer: dose

explanation:
    With no number, a headline cannot tell you much. Ask how big the dose is, and compare it with doses you know, like an average year.

item u4-l5-match-questions
type: match
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources
references: ref-who-ionizing-radiation

prompt:
    Match each question to what it asks about a dose.

pairs:
    {"id":"big","left":"How big?","right":"The amount, in millisieverts"}
    {"id":"fast","left":"How fast?","right":"All at once, or over time"}
    {"id":"cmp","left":"Compared with what?","right":"Doses you already know, like an average year"}

explanation:
    Size, speed and comparison are the three things that turn a bare number into something you can judge.

item u4-x-tom-number
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources

statement:
    A radiation number with no unit and nothing to compare it with tells you whether a dose is large.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Without a unit and a comparison, such as an average year of about 6 millisieverts, a number cannot tell you whether a dose is large.
